\title{CompPoly}
\author{dhsorens}
\date{2026-08-18}

\p{
    Computable polynomials and finite fields in Lean: an executable mirror of the Mathlib definitions, with transfer theorems so proofs can be done on the noncomputable side. I have written about why [computability matters for a specification](dhsorens-0025); this page is about the artefact.
}

\p{
    To work through:
    \ul{
        \li{The representations chosen, and which operations each makes cheap. See [univariate and multilinear polynomials](dhsorens-002M).}
        \li{The transfer pattern: computable definition, Mathlib definition, proven equivalence, and how proofs move across it.}
        \li{Field representations and the small fields that matter — see [the small-field turn](dhsorens-002K).}
        \li{What running the spec actually buys: testing a specification against a reference implementation.}
        \li{Performance in Lean, and whether it is adequate for testing at realistic trace sizes.}
        \li{Which [ArkLib](dhsorens-0040) developments depend on it.}
    }
}

\p{
    Sources: [CompPoly](https://github.com/Verified-zkEVM/CompPoly).
}
